---
<BaseLayout title="Red Rocks, a Pink Jeep and Teal Arches — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A winter weekend in Sedona, Arizona: the Broken Arrow route by Pink Jeep, Chicken Point, a moonrise over the red rocks from SR 179, and the only McDonald's with teal arches.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · January–February 2026</p>
  <h1>Red Rocks, a Pink Jeep and Teal Arches</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A winter weekend in Sedona: Broken Arrow, Chicken Point, and a moon over the red rocks.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>3 days · January 30 – February 1, 2026</strong><br />
    Lunch in West Sedona → Tlaquepaque → Broken Arrow route → White Line slickrock → Chicken Point → back along Broken Arrow →
    golden hour and moonrise from SR 179 → the teal arches in West Sedona<br />
    About ten hours of driving each way from Los Angeles ·
    One Pink Jeep · one moon, a day short of full · one pair of turquoise arches ·
    and Wangcai, the cream-colored member of the crew
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-sedona.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-sedona.webp'} alt="Map of Sedona showing the Broken Arrow route and Chicken Point south of town, the moonrise spot on SR 179 near uptown, and the McDonald's in West Sedona." /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole weekend fits in a few miles of Sedona. Each stop is placed where its photo was taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    This one is short: a single weekend and a few dozen photos. It had no grand purpose. The SIGGRAPH
    deadline had just passed, and the whole idea was to get away and relax for a couple of days. But Sedona is a place where a few dozen
    photos go a long way. The town sits in a basin at the foot of the Mogollon Rim in northern Arizona, ringed by
    buttes, spires and cliffs of sandstone. A thin coating of iron oxide stains the rock red, and the low sun turns it
    every shade between rust and flame. In winter the air is dry and clear, the sky is a deep, hard blue, and the
    contrast between the two is almost unreasonable.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: what was photographed,
    where, and at what minute. It is also one of the road trips with Wangcai, our cream-colored dog, who
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">came home in January 2025</a> and has been riding along ever since. She makes her
    appearance at the end, under a very unusual sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    We drove out on the Friday: about ten hours each way from Los Angeles, for a day and a half on the ground. Honestly, a lot
    of our road trips, this one included, would not have happened without the car's Full Self-Driving (FSD). We have
    kept it switched on since we got the car in May 2025. It does not drive the trip for you, since someone still has
    to watch the road the whole way, but it takes enough of the strain out of a ten-hour day behind the wheel that a
    weekend like this stops sounding unreasonable.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Broken Arrow by Pink Jeep</h2>
  <p>
    The main event of Saturday was a <strong>Pink Jeep tour</strong> on the <strong>Broken Arrow</strong> route, after
    lunch at <strong>Tortas de Fuego</strong> in West Sedona: a whole fried fish, a molcajete heaped with grilled
    shrimp and meat, and fried ice cream. Between lunch and the tour we walked around <strong>Tlaquepaque</strong>, where the
    chapel bell tower stands out against the red rock.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lunch-fried-fish.webp'} alt="A whole fried fish with lime and pickled onion on a green plate, with rice and a bowl of black beans topped with cheese." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fried fish at Tortas de Fuego.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tlaquepaque.webp'} alt="A chapel bell tower with a cross above bare sycamores and green shrubs, with red rock behind, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The chapel tower at Tlaquepaque.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Pink Jeep Tours has been driving visitors into the backcountry around Sedona since 1960, in open-sided Jeeps painted
    a shade of pink you could see from orbit. Broken Arrow is its signature route: a rough four-wheel-drive track that
    leaves SR 179 south of town and climbs over bare slabs of sandstone into the rocks. The route takes its name, the
    story goes, from the 1950 western <em>Broken Arrow</em>, which was filmed in the area. By 3:20 we were riding out along the
    Broken Arrow track, and at 3:40 my first photos were taken where the dirt road meets the rock.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pink-jeep-track.webp'} alt="A pink Jeep with a canopy coming straight up a red-dirt track through juniper, with red cliffs behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Another Pink Jeep on the Broken Arrow track, 3:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Wangcai sat this part out. The tour was just our group; she waited in our car with Dog Mode on, the Tesla setting that
    keeps the cabin climate-controlled while the car is parked.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'broken-arrow-spires.webp'} alt="A cluster of red sandstone towers and spires above a dirt track lined with juniper and cactus, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first stop: red towers above the dirt track.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'broken-arrow-valley.webp'} alt="A long valley of green juniper forest below banded red and white cliffs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view the other way, across a valley of juniper.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first stop already had everything Sedona is known for. On one side, a cluster of red towers rose straight out
    of the juniper, fluted and pillared like a ruined cathedral. On the other, the land fell away into a long valley of
    green forest, and beyond it ran a wall of cliffs in layers: deep red at the bottom, then orange, then pale cream at
    the top, where the younger rock caps the older. The light was still high and white, two hours before sunset,
    and the shadows in the cracks of the towers were almost black.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'broken-arrow-dome.webp'} alt="A forested red hill of rounded sandstone ledges, with a flat-topped butte behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Rounded ledges of sandstone, and a butte behind.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'broken-arrow-trailhead.webp'} alt="A trail marker in a stone cairn on red slickrock, with juniper and a valley of cliffs beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A trail marker set in a stone cairn.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few steps further on, the ground turns to <strong>slickrock</strong>, the local name for bare sandstone worn
    smooth by wind and water. It lies in great rounded ledges, stacked like the layers of a cake that has been left out
    in the rain, and the vegetation hangs on in the cracks between them. Trail markers here are posts set in little
    cylinders of stacked stone, because there is nothing to dig into. The Jeeps drive straight up and over these
    slabs, which is the whole point of the tour: the rock grips the tires far better than it looks like it should.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pink-jeep.webp'} alt="A pink open-topped Jeep with a canopy parked below tall red sandstone spires, with a second pink Jeep behind it in the brush." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Pink Jeep, parked under the spires.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    And there it was, parked under the spires: the Jeep itself. It is a Wrangler with its doors and roof replaced by
    a canopy and rows of bench seats, and the pink is even louder in person than in the brochures. Against the dark red
    rock and the gray-green junipers it looks like a toy someone has dropped into a landscape painting. Behind it, a
    second pink Jeep was nosing through the brush.
  </p>

  <h3>White Line and the slickrock</h3>
  <p>
    By 3:45 the tour had climbed onto a broad shelf of slickrock that maps label <strong>White Line</strong>. We were out
    of the Jeep for about a quarter of an hour, from the White Line shelf to the edge at Chicken Point, and most of the
    photos from it are of the group in front of the Jeep, which stay in the family album. Between them are a few frames of the rock itself: smooth, salmon-colored
    stone running down into a shaded gully, and the same cluster of spires from a new angle, now with the sun striking
    their faces and lighting them orange.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'white-line-spires.webp'} alt="A row of fluted red sandstone towers lit orange by the afternoon sun, above smooth banded slickrock." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The spires from the White Line shelf.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    What strikes you about the rock up close is the banding. The towers and the slabs underfoot are striped with thin
    horizontal layers, some a shade paler, some a shade darker, each one a few inches of sand laid down long ago when
    this was a coastal plain of dunes and tidal flats. Erosion carves the layers at different speeds, which is why the
    towers have their ribbed, stacked look and why the slickrock breaks into steps and shelves rather than smooth
    slopes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'white-line-banding.webp'} alt="Close view of a red sandstone wall in fine horizontal bands, with a thin white stripe across it and small pockmarks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The banding up close.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Chicken Point</h3>
  <p>
    At 3:50 the Jeep reached <strong>Chicken Point</strong>, one of the classic stops on the Broken Arrow route. It is a wide, tilted bench of red slickrock at the foot of a sheer cliff, with the whole
    southern half of the Sedona basin spread out below. Pink Jeeps are not the only vehicles that make it up here: a pair of off-road buggies was
    parked at the far edge of the rock.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicken-point-wall.webp'} alt="A long wall of banded red cliffs topped with pale rock, above a slope of juniper forest." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cliffs above Chicken Point.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicken-point-view.webp'} alt="A hazy view over a wide forested valley to a long flat-topped mesa on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking out over the basin.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The view from the edge is the reason the tours come here. Below the rock lies a forest of juniper and pinyon so
    dense it looks like moss, and beyond it, across miles of haze, a long flat-topped mesa runs along the horizon.
    The air out there was soft and blue with distance, the kind of layered, receding blue that landscape painters use
    to say "far away". Up close everything was red and sharp; in the distance everything was blue and blurred, and the
    two halves of the picture hardly seemed to belong to the same afternoon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chicken-point-sun.webp'} alt="The sun blazing low in a pale sky beside the dark silhouette of a sandstone fin, with the mesa and forest below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sun beside the fin at Chicken Point, 3:52 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Turn around and the light changes completely. Chicken Point faces the winter sun, and just before four o'clock it hung low
    over the basin, blazing in a pale sky streaked with a contrail. Next to it, a tall fin of sandstone went fully
    black against the glare, with only its rim catching light. The mesa on the horizon flattened into a silhouette.
    Photos taken straight into the sun rarely work, but this one does, because the rock gives it a hard edge to push
    against.
  </p>

  <h3>The way back</h3>
  <p>
    By 4:14 the Jeep was heading back north along the <strong>Broken Arrow Trail</strong>. The sun was dropping
    behind the rocks now, and the slickrock benches lay in their shadow, cool and gray-pink. From one of them, a red
    spire rose above a slope of juniper, against a sky that was beginning to pale. And
    down on the rock below it, tiny, sat a pink Jeep: the best possible scale bar for how big these formations
    really are.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'broken-arrow-spire-jeep.webp'} alt="A red sandstone spire and cliffs above a slope of juniper, with a tiny pink Jeep on the pale slickrock in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Find the Jeep.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'broken-arrow-dusk.webp'} alt="Shaded gray slickrock in the foreground with a flat-topped butte and a red spire silhouetted beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Late-afternoon shadow on the slickrock.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The widest view of the tour came at 4:18. From an opening in the trees, the whole Sedona skyline lay
    across the middle of the frame, a long row of red buttes and spires with the cream-colored cliffs of the Rim
    behind them, all of it still in full sun. The foreground, meanwhile, was already in shade, dark pines framing the
    view like a window. In winter, the light leaves the low ground first and climbs the rocks as the sun goes down.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'basin-view-wide.webp'} alt="Dark pines in shade framing a wide view across a juniper forest to a long line of sunlit red buttes and the cream cliffs of the Rim." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The skyline of buttes from the Broken Arrow Trail, 4:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Golden hour and a moonrise on SR 179</h2>
  <p>
    That climb of the light is what Sedona is famous for, and an hour later the photos pick it up again. At 5:29 the
    camera was on <strong>SR 179</strong> near uptown Sedona, by the shops at Hyatt Piñon Pointe, looking across
    the road and a low wall to the rocks beyond. The valley floor was already in shadow. Only
    the tops of the buttes were still in the sun, and they had gone from red to a deep, glowing orange.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sr179-golden.webp'} alt="Red buttes glowing orange in the last sun above a shadowed valley, seen across a road and a low wall with railings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Golden hour from SR 179, 5:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is a strange sight the first time you see it. The rock seems to be lit from inside, as if it were giving off
    light rather than reflecting it. Part of that is the color of the sunlight itself, which near sunset has lost most
    of its blue to the long path through the atmosphere. Part of it is the rock, whose iron-red surface throws that
    warm light back almost undiluted. And part of it is contrast: the sky behind is cool and blue, the ground below is
    in shadow, and the rocks sit between them like embers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the moon came up. On January 31 it was a day short of full: the February full moon fell on the 1st. A
    nearly full moon rises around sunset, on the opposite side of the sky from the sun, and in Sedona that means it
    climbs out from behind the eastern rocks just as they catch the last light. At 5:30 it was already clear of the
    ridge, a pale white disk hanging beside a row of glowing spires (possibly Snoopy Rock).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'moonrise-wide.webp'} alt="A nearly full moon rising beside a red ridge of spires glowing in the sunset, above a fence along the road." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Moonrise over the red rocks, 5:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'moonrise.webp'} alt="Close view of the white moon in a blue sky beside red sandstone spires lit orange by the setting sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A minute later, zoomed in.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The zoomed-in frame from a minute later is the best photo of the weekend. The spires are lit a burning orange,
    every ledge and crack picked out by the low sun. The juniper on the slope below is dotted dark green against red
    soil, and the moon sits off to one side in a flawless blue sky, round and white and a little unreal, like a
    lamp someone has hung there on purpose. It lasted a few minutes. Then the sun dropped and the color drained out of
    the rock from the bottom up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The last frames of the day, at 5:34, were taken a few hundred yards down the road. A red butte was still catching the final light, framed between two tall, dark cypress trees. Below it, in the
    valley, the evening had already arrived. Around six we had dinner on the terrace at Sound Bites Grill,
    looking out at the red rocks, and finished with ice cream from the
    Black Cow Cafe.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'last-light-cypress.webp'} alt="A red rock butte glowing in the last sunlight, framed between two tall dark cypress trees under a pale blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last light, 5:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dinner-terrace.webp'} alt="The view from a restaurant terrace under a white umbrella: red buttes and the pale cliffs of the Rim above a forested valley at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from dinner, 5:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: The teal arches</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday morning has exactly one stop in my camera roll, and it is a McDonald's. Not just any McDonald's: the one
    on SR 89A in West Sedona is famous as the only one in the world whose arches are <strong>turquoise</strong>
    instead of gold. When it opened in 1993, the city did not want a bright yellow sign competing with the red rocks.
    Sedona's design rules keep buildings in earth tones that blend into the landscape, so the company agreed to swap the
    gold for teal.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'teal-arches.webp'} alt="Turquoise McDonald's arches mounted on a terracotta-red wall of a building, with a teal umbrella below, against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The world's only teal arches, West Sedona, 10:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The result is oddly elegant. The arches are mounted on a tall terracotta-red tower that matches the color of the
    rocks, next to a wall of sand-colored stucco, and the teal picks up the color of the umbrellas on the patio below.
    Seen against a winter sky, it looks less like a fast-food sign than a piece of Southwestern design. It has turned
    into a landmark in its own right: plenty of people come here for the photo rather than the food, and the
    camera roll, tellingly, has no pictures of the food.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which brings us to Wangcai. Fifteen photos were taken under the arches in the space of nine seconds, and in every
    one of them she is sitting on the patio in the sun, her cream coat almost white in the hard Arizona light,
    framed against the red wall. In some of them she is looking up at something just out of the frame, ears high,
    with the expression of a dog who suspects there may be food involved.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-sedona.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog sitting upright on a patio against a red wall, looking up, with a pink leash." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, under the teal arches.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    My photos end there, at 10:34 on Sunday morning. After that came the long drive home to Los Angeles, which is
    not in the camera roll. As weekends go, it
    was a small one: one afternoon in the rocks, one moonrise, and one very unusual McDonald's. But it is hard to think
    of a better way to spend a winter weekend than watching a full moon come up over a ridge of glowing red stone, and
    then, the next morning, introducing the dog to a pair of turquoise arches.
  </p>
